# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL in the default (bridge) mode. JARVIS runs
# perfectly with an empty file — the bridge authenticates off your Claude Code
# login, and voice input and output both fall back to the browser's own speech.
# Copy this to .env.local, and add only what you actually want to change.
#
# The one line worth adding is ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, down in the BRIDGE section:
# it gives you a much better voice and Scribe transcription. The free tier is
# plenty for a demo. Never commit a real key.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* and
#     ELEVENLABS_API_KEY variables from its shell environment.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* variables from .env.local.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser.


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — read from the shell that runs `npm run bridge`
# ===================================================================
# These are shown with their defaults. Leave them unset to accept the default.

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Interface the bridge listens on. Default 127.0.0.1 — this machine only.
# Setting anything else puts the agent on your network; don't, unless you have
# put your own authentication in front of it.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Extra hostnames the bridge answers to, comma separated. Requests addressed to
# any other name are refused, which is what stops DNS rebinding. Default: none
# beyond localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1].
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# The model the bridge asks Claude Code to run. Default claude-opus-5.
# JARVIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Reasoning effort. Default medium.
# JARVIS_EFFORT=medium

# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` turns
# it on for you (via --writes). Read the decideTool gate in bridge/policy.mjs first.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Which MCP servers from ~/.claude.json JARVIS gets, comma separated. Unset
# passes every configured server; an empty value passes none. Each one is
# something a voice agent that reads web pages can be talked into calling, so
# name only what you use. Default: all.
# JARVIS_MCP_SERVERS=

# Let the connectors attached to your claude.ai account (mail, drive,
# calendar and other account connectors) into the session too. Off by default. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ACCOUNT_CONNECTORS=0

# Built-in capabilities to leave out, comma separated: browser (your own
# signed-in Chrome), camera, interface (the ui_* retinting tools), youtube,
# tiktok, instagram, facebook, studio, crypto, autopilot, pipelines, agency, system. Left-out
# tools never reach the model, which also trims every turn's input. Default:
# none.
# JARVIS_DISABLE=

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# The agent's working directory. Its file tools (Read, Grep, Glob, and Write /
# Edit when writes are on) are confined to this folder, the system temp
# directory and JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS. Credential locations (~/.ssh, ~/.claude,
# .env files …) are refused even inside those. Default: ~/.jarvis/workspace.
# JARVIS_WORKSPACE=

# Extra directories the file tools may use and the /file endpoint may serve
# images from, comma separated — a Pictures folder, an external drive of
# footage. Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=

# Keep a transcript of every session, the way Claude Code does, under
# ~/.claude/projects. Off by default: nothing reads them back, and they hold
# every tool result verbatim. Default 0.
# JARVIS_KEEP_TRANSCRIPTS=0

# Run the agent (the Claude Code CLI the SDK starts) through this executable
# instead of the bundled one — typically a sandbox wrapper that execs the real
# CLI with the same arguments. Every built-in tool runs inside that process,
# so this is where OS-level confinement belongs. Default: the bundled CLI.
# JARVIS_CLAUDE_EXECUTABLE=

# Log every message the agent sends, including answer text, tool calls and the
# MCP servers that connected, to the bridge's terminal. For debugging only.
# Default 0.
# JARVIS_DEBUG=0

# WSL only: the name of the Windows pipe the browser relay connects to.
# Default: claude-mcp-browser-bridge- followed by your Windows user name.
# JARVIS_CHROME_PIPE=

# ElevenLabs voice id for the bridge's own speech endpoint. Default: George.
# Studio voiceovers don't use it: they use Kokoro (JARVIS_KOKORO_VOICE below).
# JARVIS_VOICE_ID=JBFqnCBsd6RMkjVDRZzb

# ElevenLabs API key. Optional. With it, JARVIS speaks in the ElevenLabs voice
# and transcribes with ElevenLabs Scribe; without it, everything still works on
# the browser's own speech. If you already have an elevenlabs MCP server in
# ~/.claude.json, the bridge reads the key from there and you can leave this
# blank. Default: none.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Which brain the browser talks to. Default bridge.
#   bridge — talk to the local bridge (recommended). No API key, and every MCP
#            server in your Claude Code config is available.
#   direct — the browser calls the Claude API itself. Needs VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# VITE_BACKEND=bridge

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine, used when no ElevenLabs voice is active.
# Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, slower to start.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Prefer the ElevenLabs voice from the browser when a key is available.
# Default false.
# VITE_USE_ELEVENLABS=false

# HUD layout. Default command.
#   command — the Command Center: panels for system, tasks, agents, business,
#             leads, YouTube and activity around the reactor, plus a command box.
#   classic — the original minimal HUD.
# VITE_HUD_LAYOUT=command

# Anthropic API key — DIRECT MODE ONLY. Required if and only if
# VITE_BACKEND=direct, and it ships to the browser, so use it only for a local
# demo. The default bridge mode never reads it. Default: none.
# VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# ===================================================================
# CREATOR SUITE (bridge) — see CREATOR.md, YOUTUBE.md, SOCIAL.md
# ===================================================================
# Platform sign-ins are not env vars: run `npm run youtube:connect` and
# `npm run social:connect -- tiktok|instagram|facebook`. Add `--account <name>`
# to either for another channel's account (SOCIAL.md). Tokens are saved under
# ~/.jarvis/secrets, which JARVIS's own file tools cannot read.

# Your PUBLIC receiving addresses, for watch-only balances and payment QR codes.
# Chains: eth, base, btc, sol. Never put a private key or seed phrase anywhere.
# JARVIS_WALLETS=eth:0xYourAddress,btc:bc1YourAddress,sol:YourSolAddress

# Optional RPC endpoints (public ones are used by default).
# ETH_RPC_URL=
# BASE_RPC_URL=
# SOL_RPC_URL=

# Optional: pin NFT packages to IPFS through Pinata.
# PINATA_JWT=

# Studio voiceover voice (Kokoro, free, runs on this laptop; the ~90 MB model
# downloads to ~/.jarvis/models on first use). Default bm_george. Others:
# bm_fable, bm_lewis, bm_daniel, am_michael, am_fenrir, af_heart.
# JARVIS_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Phone line for the agency loop to call you on (Twilio; a free trial works).
# Easier: `npm run phone:connect` saves these to ~/.jarvis/secrets. Without a
# line, JARVIS shows a Windows notification instead.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=

# Optional: a Pollinations API token for the create_image tool (sent as a
# Bearer token). Free pictures work without it.
# POLLINATIONS_TOKEN=

# Optional: ffmpeg location if it is not on PATH.
# FFMPEG_PATH=
# FFPROBE_PATH=

# Optional: Instagram Graph API version. Default v24.0.
# IG_GRAPH_VERSION=v24.0

# Optional: Facebook Graph API version for Page Reels. Default v24.0.
# FB_GRAPH_VERSION=v24.0

# YouTube uploads a day across ALL channels. Google's default API quota
# (10,000 units; ~1,600 per upload) allows about 6. Raise it only if Google
# grants you more quota. Default 6.
# JARVIS_YOUTUBE_DAILY_UPLOADS=6

# Effort for the autopilot's unattended runs (live voice uses JARVIS_EFFORT).
# JARVIS_AUTOPILOT_EFFORT=high
# Where Ollama listens, for the local brain.
# OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
